{# ============================================================
   THE LANGUAGE SWITCHER

   A dropdown: a button showing the language you are reading, and a menu of
   every language THIS page was built in. Rendered from `alternates`, the same
   list the hreflang block uses, so the switcher can never offer a page that
   does not exist: a language appears here the moment site.json lists it for
   this page, and not before.

   WHY A DROPDOWN AND NOT A ROW OF OPTIONS
   Two languages fit side by side; five do not, and the row would either wrap
   the topbar onto a second line or push the back button off the edge. The
   dropdown is the shape that survives the fourth language without anyone
   revisiting it.

   WHY <details> AND NOT A SCRIPTED MENU
   It opens, closes and takes keyboard focus with no JavaScript at all, which
   matters for a control that sits in the topbar of a page whose scripts have
   not finished loading. It is also already the site's disclosure element:
   the FAQ is built from it, so the chevron and the marker reset below are the
   same treatment. shared/lang-switch.js adds only outside-tap and Escape,
   and the menu works without it.

   WHERE IT GOES, AND WHY NOT EVERYWHERE (#139)
   Only on the hub and on a game's HOME screen. Switching language mid-room is
   not a thing: everyone in a room shares one secret word and one interface,
   so the language is the ROOM's, fixed when it is created (#138). A control
   that looks like it would change it, in a place where it cannot, is worse
   than no control.

   On the hub it sits at the LEFT of the top bar, opposite the account button.
   On a game page it sits at the right, opposite the back link. Either way it
   is the outer item on its side, and the menu aligns to that same edge so it
   never opens off-screen; see .home-topbar .lang-menu in base.css.

   The current language is a <span>, not a link. It is a state indicator;
   making it an anchor to the page you are already on adds a self-referencing
   link for a crawler to weigh and a keyboard tab stop that does nothing.
   ============================================================ #}
{% macro langSwitch(alternates, label) %}
{% if alternates.length > 1 %}
<details class="lang-switch">
  <summary aria-label="{{ label }}">
    <svg class="lang-globe" width="15" height="15" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8"/><path d="M3 12h18" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round"/><ellipse cx="12" cy="12" rx="4.2" ry="9" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8"/></svg>
{% for a in alternates %}
{% if a.current %}
    <span class="lang-current" lang="{{ a.lang }}">{{ a.label }}</span>
{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
  </summary>
  <ul class="lang-menu">
{% for a in alternates %}
{% if a.current %}
    <li><span class="lang-opt is-current" lang="{{ a.lang }}" aria-current="true">{{ a.label }}</span></li>
{% else %}
    <li><a class="lang-opt" lang="{{ a.lang }}" hreflang="{{ a.lang }}" href="{{ a.path }}">{{ a.label }}</a></li>
{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
  </ul>
</details>
{# Ships with the component rather than from each page, so adding the switcher
   to a fourth page is one macro call and nothing else to remember. Deferred,
   and the menu is fully usable before it arrives. #}
<script src="/shared/lang-switch.js" defer></script>
{% endif %}
{% endmacro %}
